  "pages": [
    {
      "idx": 0,
      "label": "1",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: A handoff means passing a smaller\ntask to a helper model and getting its\nanswer back. Last time, we taught a basic\nPython routine. Now we study a routine\nusing helpers and a separate reading test.\n\nFOCUS: Can changing the RLM program make\ncomplete solutions more reliable?\n\nCAUTION: The training and matching results\nare separate studies, not one learning\ncurve."
    },
    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: We want each user's count of\nquestions asking for a location. Oslo and\nKyoto qualify; Hamlet asks for a person.\nHelpers identify question types; Python\ncounts: Ada one, Ben one.\n\nWHY: The main model can inspect a file\nwithout reading it all at once. Helpers\ncan divide work again.\n\nCAUTION: This invented example explains\nthe workflow, not a demonstrated ability\nto plan any unfamiliar task."
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Read the example left to right. The model\nsees a counting task and three helper\nreplies. It is trained to produce code\nthat counts the two location replies. SFT\nchanges its learned parameters, not just\nits prompt.\n\nThis illustrates one step. Full examples\nalso demonstrate asking the helper and\nreturning the calculated answer.\n\nTwo copies started from the same model.\nEach learned from 72 worked interactions,\nusing different examples and an unchanged\nhelper."
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "The bars show confirmed successes: 12 for\nthe starting model, 55 for one trained\ncopy, and 53 for the other, out of 72\ntasks.\n\nSeven starting-model results and two\nsecond-copy results are unknown. They\ncould raise the totals to 19 and 55,\nrespectively, but cannot erase the\ntraining advantage.\n\nBoth copies started from the same model.\nThese are separate training runs, not\nsuccessive stages of one run."
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: This is a separate reading test. Maya\nbought a red bike: a vehicle follows from\nthe text; a blue bike contradicts it. A\nname links each statement to its answer.\n\nIF ASKED: The real task also allows not\nenough information. Software supplies\nnames and format, not correct answers.\nWithout names, order supplies the link.\n\nTRANSITION: Does this help when one\nrequest contains many statements?"
    },
    {
      "idx": 5,
      "label": "6",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Qwen and Mistral are two language models.\nMoving right means more statements in each\nrequest. At 64, matching names raised Qwen\nfrom 44% to 83%, and Mistral from 35% to\n52%.\n\nBoth formats used the same 16 sets of\ntexts. Three Mistral replies broke the\nrequired format and were counted as wrong.\n\nThis measures helper judgments, not\ncomplete RLM solutions. Backup 6 also\nshows matching row numbers."
    },
    {
      "idx": 6,
      "label": "7",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: All methods judged the same 768\nstatements. Each time covers that whole\nworkload, not one request. Large named\nrequests scored 85% in 91 seconds; one\nstatement at a time scored 87% in 31\nseconds.\n\nIF ASKED: Up to four requests ran at once.\nSixteen per request was faster but less\naccurate.\n\nCAUTION: One-at-a-time sent about 2.5\ntimes as much input as large named\nrequests. This is a local tradeoff."
    },
    {
      "idx": 7,
      "label": "8",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "The proposed component groups input items\nand matches each returned answer to its\nsource. The main model still decides what\nto ask.\n\nToday, the program matches a whole request\nto its response. We want it to track\nindividual items inside that response,\ntoo. First compare fixed group sizes;\nchoosing sizes automatically comes later.\n\nWe have not shown better complete\nsolutions. Even perfectly matched answers\ncan be wrong."
    },
    {
      "idx": 8,
      "label": "9",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: Training improved a routine; matching\nnames improved helper reading. I recommend\na focused whole-task study of the handoff\nprocedure.\n\nDISCUSS: Is this a worthwhile\ncontribution? Which realistic task would\nmake a convincing test?\n\nSTOP: This ends the ten-minute main talk.\nRemaining slides are optional answers to\nquestions, not more required presentation."
    },
    {
      "idx": 9,
      "label": "10",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: Count only points attached to\nquestions asking for a location. Ada gets\nfour plus three, or seven. Ben gets two,\nnot the six attached to his person\nquestion. Only Ada exceeds five: one user.\n\nIF ASKED: The helper classifies; Python\nadds and compares. Wrong classifications\ncan spoil correct arithmetic.\n\nCAUTION: This is invented, not a copied\ntraining example. Assigned points are\ncalled weights in the experiment."
    },
    {
      "idx": 10,
      "label": "11",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "This asks whether adding any names helps,\nor whether the names must match. Both\nversions had names and valid formatting.\nMatching raised accuracy from 32.6% to\n78.8%.\n\nAnswer order still told the model which\nstatement to judge, even when the names\ndiffered.\n\nThis plot scores only the last 32\njudgments in each request of 48\nstatements. It uses different texts from\nslide 6, so compare the two bars here, not\nacross slides."
    },
    {
      "idx": 11,
      "label": "12",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Unlike SFT, these runs learned from scores\non attempted solutions. One rewarded\ncorrect final answers. The other also\nrewarded agreement with Python's\ncalculation from helper replies.\n\nThe middle bar shows 55 confirmed\nsuccesses. Two results are unknown, so its\ntotal could reach 57, matching the\nstarting model.\n\nEach run attempted 576 practice solutions\nbefore testing. These scores check final\nanswers only; reviewing the actual\ncalculations is unfinished."
    },
    {
      "idx": 12,
      "label": "13",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "First find users who asked a location\nquestion. Then count those users' number\nquestions. The instructions supplied both\nsteps. This tests carrying out a new\ncombination, not inventing the plan.\n\nThere were two confirmed successes before\nSFT and 29 after it. Eleven earlier\nresults and one later result are unknown.\nEven if all eleven earlier results\nsucceeded, training would still be ahead.\n\nWe checked the calculation as well as the\nfinal answer."
    },
    {
      "idx": 13,
      "label": "14",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: Imagine being told to answer row\nseven but label it row eight. That name\npoints to another text in the request.\nAccuracy was 39% with such misleading\nnames, versus 80% with unrelated names.\n\nIF ASKED: Order still told the model which\nstatement to judge. Software forced the\nanswer names.\n\nCAUTION: Deliberate stress test, not\nnormal use. We observed output behavior,\nnot the model's attention."
    },
    {
      "idx": 14,
      "label": "15",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "This adds matching row numbers to the\ncomparison on slide 6. Both row numbers\nand arbitrary names helped.\n\nAt 64 statements, Qwen scored 44% without\nnames, 85% with row numbers, and 83% with\narbitrary names. Mistral scored 35%, 58%,\nand 52%, respectively.\n\nThree Mistral replies with arbitrary names\nbroke the required format. The difference\nbetween naming styles therefore does not\nmeasure reading ability alone."
    }
